hey this is preshtalwalker  start with the unit square  we'll create a new rectangle as follows  we'll attach a rectangle of area one to  the side  we will then attach a rectangle of area  1 to the top  the border of the shape is a new  rectangle  we'll label our starting rectangle r sub  1.  the new rectangle we created will be  labeled r sub 2.  we'll now repeat this process starting  with r sub 2.  we'll create a new rectangle by  attaching a rectangle of area 1 to its  side then we'll attach a rectangle area  1 to its top  this creates the next rectangle r sub 3.  we can repeat this process indefinitely  starting with rectangle r sub n minus 1  we can create a new rectangle by  attaching a rectangle of area 1 to its  side and then a rectangle of area 1 to  its top  this creates the next rectangle r sub n  each of these rectangles has a length  and a width  the question is  what is the ratio of the length to the  width of r sub n as n goes to infinity  in other words what is the limit as n  goes to infinity of l sub n divided by w  sub n  give this problem a try and when you're  ready keep watching the video for the  solution  we'll get started with our first  rectangle which is a unit square  the length and the width of this  rectangle are 1.  what happens in the next step of the  process  we first attach a rectangle of area 1 to  the side  we know the width of the original  rectangle is one  therefore the length of this new  rectangle has to be one divided by one  in order that the area be equal to one  we now attach a rectangle with area one  to the top  this new rectangle  has a length  that's equal to two  in order for the area to be equal to one  the length the width of this rectangle  has to be equal to one divided by two  we therefore have a rectangle r sub two  where its length is equal to two and its  width is equal to three  halves the ratio in this rectangle is  equal to two divided by three halves  which is equal to two over one times two  over three  what happens for the general case we  start with the rectangle r sub n  this rectangle has a width and a length  we first attach a rectangle of area one  to the side  the width of this rectangle is equal to  w sub n  so its length has to be equal to 1  divided by w sub n so that its area is  equal to 1.  this gives us a formula that the next  length of the next rectangle is equal to  the previous length plus one divided by  the previous width  we then attach a rectangle of area one  to the top  now the length of this rectangle is  equal to l sub n plus 1 over w sub n  in order that the area be equal to 1  we need the width of this rectangle to  be the reciprocal of this quantity  now we can simplify this fraction by  multiplying by w sub n  we now have a term l sub n w sub n  this is equal to the length times the  width of rectangle n  in other words this term is the area of  rectangle r sub n  the first rectangle was a unit square  which had area one  the next rectangle had two rectangles of  area one added to it so its total area  would have been three  and in general  the area of r sub n will be equal to 2  times n minus 1 because each time we  take a step in this process we're adding  two units to the area  so we can substitute in 2n minus 1 for  this term l sub n times w sub n  we end up with the formula  w sub n divided by two n  so this is the amount that the width  grows in the new rectangle which we need  to add to the previous width  we now have formulas for the length and  the width of the new rectangle in terms  of the length and the width of the  previous rectangle  we now want to compute the ratio of the  length to the width  we'll use our recursive formulas  we can simplify this fraction by  multiplying the numerator and  denominator by 2n times w sub n  we again have the term  l sub n times w sub n which is the area  of rectangle r sub n  we can simplify this formula  and now we have one more term we need to  simplify this is the width of rectangle  n squared  we can rewrite this in terms of things  we already know and care about  the width of rectangle n squared is  equal to the area of rectangle n divided  by the ratio of the length to the width  of rectangle n  if we substitute this in  we have the area being equal to two n  minus one  so putting it back into our main  fraction  we end up getting the new ratio of is  equal to the ratio of the length to the  width of rectangle n  times two fractions  we have two n divided by two n minus one  and then we have two n divided by two n  plus one  so let's compute a few of the terms  using this recursive formula  we start out with the unit square which  has a ratio of its length to its width  of one  the next rectangle will have a ratio  that's two over one times two over three  times the previous ratio of one  we can omit this term of one because it  won't affect the ultimate product  the next rectangle will have a ratio  that's four over three times four over  five times the previous ratio  the next rectangle will have a ratio  that's 6 over 5 times 6 over 7 times the  previous ratio  this pattern will continue indefinitely  the ratio of the length to the width as  n goes to infinity will therefore be an  infinite product  we have the product of the fractions 2k  over 2k minus 1 times 2k over 2k plus 1  where k runs from 1 to infinity  what does this evaluate to  this is a famous formula and it exactly  equals pi divided by 2 which is  approximately 1.571  this is known as the wallace product  formula for pi and it was first  described in 1655.  i know some of you will be curious about  how to prove this formula and you can  check out a video i made on this i  provided a link in the video description  and it's an interesting example of how  you can use calculus to prove this  formula  so we end up with a very surprising  answer we started out with the square  and we kept adding rectangles to its  side and to the top  we ended up creating a rectangle whose  ratio of its length to its width is  actually related to pi and i find that  quite interesting that you can start out  with just rectangles and you end up with  pi  did you figure this out  thanks for watching this video please  subscribe to my channel i make videos on  math and game theory you can catch me on  my blog mind your decisions which you  can follow on facebook google plus and  patreon you can catch me on social media  at preshtalwalker and if you like this  video please check out my books there  are links in the video description

